# Local Development Deployment

- Requests to deploy locally mean updating the local development app for rapid iteration. Use the active feature source, including uncommitted changes when appropriate.
- For PHP/CSS/JavaScript source changes, use the development bind mounts, rebuild only the affected generated assets and their cache manifest, and refresh the local page. Rebuild or restart containers only when dependencies, runtime configuration, or image contents require it.
- Run focused checks and verify the actual local page, including the requested visual behavior. Report the URL and distinguish a sample demo from the running application.
- After pulling, switching branches, or changing a source-bound local deployment, run `python3 scripts/update_local_development.py --check` before reporting it ready. This checks the primary and every ready local member Account, canonical migration checksums, recorded grants, and running service health; a healthy primary alone is insufficient.
- When an authorized local update finds pending migrations or stale grants, run `python3 scripts/update_local_development.py --apply`. It preserves the existing containers and networks, pauses only affected Account writers, creates encrypted restore-verified backups for schema changes, rehearses the canonical migrations on those copies, then applies migrations and grants and verifies service health. Do not substitute `up --no-deps`, a page refresh, or manual ledger edits for this step.
- Local previews do not require a version bump, commit, remote push, PR merge, final-main CI, release images/tags, deployment notice, or countdown. PR administration can continue separately without delaying the preview. Preserve protected branch checks when merging.
- Keep the local database and other projects intact. Do not run a database upgrade or a full backup/restore rehearsal for an ordinary UI update. The existing backup directive still applies to major schema changes or database-version changes.
- Production deployment on s1 remains subject to the established release workflow and requires an explicit deployment request.
